WEBVTT

00:00:01.740 --> 00:00:03.515
Hey guys, it's Jeff Meacham.
大家好，我是杰夫·米查姆。

00:00:03.515 --> 00:00:06.750
I played the wedding DJ in the season three finale.
我在第三季结局中扮演婚礼 DJ。

00:00:06.750 --> 00:00:08.145
I believe it was 2007.
我相信那是2007年。

00:00:08.145 --> 00:00:10.175
I had just moved to Los Angeles.
我刚刚搬到洛杉矶。

00:00:10.175 --> 00:00:13.093
It was the second job I ever booked out in LA.
这是我在洛杉矶预订的第二份工作。

00:00:13.093 --> 00:00:15.820
First comedy, I had booked first multi-cam.
第一部喜剧，我预订了第一个多机位。

00:00:15.820 --> 00:00:21.972
And as soon as I got onto set, I was like, oh, this is what I want to do for the rest of my life.
当我一进入片场，我就想，哦，这就是我余生想做的事情。

00:00:21.972 --> 00:00:22.923
And here's why.
原因如下。

00:00:22.923 --> 00:00:23.938
We get onto set.
我们进入片场。

00:00:23.938 --> 00:00:25.334
It's this wedding set.
就是这套婚纱照。

00:00:25.334 --> 00:00:27.109
There's a lot of background.
有很多背景。

00:00:27.109 --> 00:00:29.266
There's all sorts of moving parts.
有各种各样的活动部件。

00:00:29.266 --> 00:00:31.739
And I'm up in the kind of a bird's eye.
我就像鸟瞰一样。

00:00:31.739 --> 00:00:44.420
to you in the DJ booth and boom, first team comes in and you guys are all carrying your coffees and your sides and your cracking jokes and having the best time together and Pam comes in and kind of orchestrates the whole scene.
DJ 台上的音乐响起，第一支队伍进来了，你们都端着咖啡、配菜和笑话，一起度过了最美好的时光，帕姆进来了，安排了整个场景。

00:00:44.420 --> 00:01:03.899
Neil's character comes and stands next to me and then you guys were hers and someone just yells the word suck a team and you guys just kind of funnel off together, leave, grab your coffees and you're giggling as you're leaving an income to this group of people that kind of look like you, wearing chalkboards with your character's names on them.
尼尔扮演的角色走过来站在我旁边，然后你们就是她的了，有人大喊“烂队”这个词，你们就聚在一起，离开，喝杯咖啡，一边咯咯地笑，一边给这群看起来像你的人留下收入，戴着写有你角色名字的黑板。

00:01:03.899 --> 00:01:05.379
And I was like, what is this?
我当时想，这是什么？

00:01:05.379 --> 00:01:07.140
And somebody comes over and they're like, oh yeah, yeah.
有人走过来，他们说，哦，是的，是的。

00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:11.500
They basically come in rehearsing and then they leave and they get to go have the best time of their lives.
他们基本上是排练然后离开，然后开始享受一生中最美好的时光。

00:01:11.500 --> 00:01:15.140
Well, these people come in and do all the blocking and get everything set with the lights.
好吧，这些人进来，做所有的阻挡工作，并把一切都设置好灯光。

00:01:15.140 --> 00:01:17.180
And then they come back in and tell jokes and be stars.
然后他们回来讲笑话并成为明星。

00:01:17.180 --> 00:01:20.700
And I was like, oh, that's what I want to do for the rest of my life.
我当时想，哦，这就是我余生想做的事。

00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:26.459
So that really was a big turning point in my life where I was like, oh, this is amazing.
所以这确实是我人生中的一个重大转折点，我当时想，哦，这太棒了。

00:01:26.459 --> 00:01:27.299
I remember that.
我记得。

00:01:27.299 --> 00:01:28.379
I remember how kind Pam was.
我记得帕姆是多么友善。

00:01:28.379 --> 00:01:38.620
In fact, I just got to work with her again recently, and how cool it was as this young 20-something where Neil comes over and stands next to me.
事实上，我最近刚刚再次与她合作，作为一个 20 多岁的年轻女孩，尼尔走过来站在我旁边，这真是太酷了。

00:01:38.620 --> 00:01:39.939
I was like, hey, man, I grew up watching you.
我当时想，嘿，伙计，我是看着你长大的。

00:01:39.939 --> 00:01:40.780
You're amazing.
你太棒了。

00:01:40.780 --> 00:01:41.620
And he's like, thanks.
他说，谢谢。

00:01:41.620 --> 00:01:49.540
I was like, oh, also, do you remember on Law & Order a couple of years ago where you played that kind of psycho killer guy where you lobotomized women by pouring hot water into their brains?
我当时想，哦，还有，你还记得几年前在《法律与秩序》中，你扮演了那种变态杀手，通过将热水倒入女性的大脑来对她们进行脑白质切除术吗？

00:01:49.540 --> 00:01:51.260
And he was like, yeah, I was like, my wife was that woman.
他说，是的，我说，我的妻子就是那个女人。

00:01:51.260 --> 00:01:53.219
And he was like, cool, I was like, cool.
他就像，酷，我就像，酷。

00:01:53.219 --> 00:01:55.340
So there you go.
所以就这样吧。

00:01:55.340 --> 00:01:56.700
incredible show to be on.
令人难以置信的表演即将上演。

00:01:56.700 --> 00:02:00.620
You guys were the best and I can't thank you enough for that experience.
你们是最棒的，我对你们的经历感激不尽。

00:02:20.860 --> 00:02:22.559
Well, thank you, Jeff.
好吧，谢谢你，杰夫。

00:02:22.559 --> 00:02:27.580
What a great note to start the show with and what a voice he has.
节目的开头真是太好了，他的声音也很棒。

00:02:27.580 --> 00:02:29.369
I hope he has his own podcast, right?
我希望他有自己的播客，对吗？

00:02:29.369 --> 00:02:30.577
Let's get a little voice.
让我们发出一点声音。

00:02:30.577 --> 00:02:32.511
Alec has done podcasts with Jeff, right?
亚历克和杰夫一起做过播客，对吧？

00:02:32.511 --> 00:02:33.188
Their buddies.
他们的伙伴。

00:02:33.188 --> 00:02:33.688
Oh, Jeff.
哦，杰夫。

00:02:33.688 --> 00:02:34.188
Yes, Jeff.
是的，杰夫。

00:02:34.188 --> 00:02:36.461
And I have a podcast that's meeting Tom Cruise.
我有一个与汤姆·克鲁斯会面的播客。

00:02:36.379 --> 00:02:37.460
if you can go listen to that too.
如果你也可以去听听的话。

00:02:37.460 --> 00:02:40.379
Yeah, that's awesome, that's a great story, Jeff.
是的，那太棒了，这是一个很棒的故事，杰夫。

00:02:40.379 --> 00:02:50.099
I'm so glad that I didn't know that little bit you did, being in that one episode, this episode, something borrowed, meant so much to you and it was such an early part of your journey.
我很高兴我不知道你做了那么一点点，在那一集中，这一集，借来的东西，对你来说意义重大，这是你旅程的早期部分。

00:02:50.099 --> 00:02:54.180
That's one of the many things, you don't know, you don't know what it means in the moment.
这是众多事情之一，你不知道，你不知道那一刻意味着什么。

00:02:54.180 --> 00:03:09.259
It's good to, when you're starting out in a business as crazy and unpredictable, as the one we're in, it's really good to have good early formative experiences, where you see functional working set, where people are happy, it's fun to tell stories, It's fun to be there, so we're glad we were able to provide that.
当你刚开始从事像我们这样疯狂和不可预测的业务时，拥有良好的早期形成性经验真的很好，你可以看到功能齐全的工作环境，人们很高兴，讲故事很有趣，在那里很有趣，所以我们很高兴能够提供这些。

00:03:09.259 --> 00:03:10.259
They're not all like that.
他们并不都是这样。

00:03:10.259 --> 00:03:13.180
Not every set is like that, and yeah, that's cool.
并不是每组都是这样，是的，这很酷。

00:03:13.180 --> 00:03:14.180
Thank you.
谢谢。

00:03:14.180 --> 00:03:28.340
Well, we're thrilled you could join us, and we're thrilled you who are listening to this could join us here today for another episode of How We Made Your Mother, where Craig Thomas and I talk about the TV show we made together or How I Met Your Mother.
好吧，我们很高兴您能加入我们，我们很高兴正在收听此节目的您今天可以加入我们的另一集《我们如何创造了你的母亲》，其中克雷格·托马斯和我谈论了我们一起制作的电视节目《我如何遇见你的母亲》。

00:03:28.340 --> 00:03:32.259
I played Ted Mosby on that show from 2005 to 2014.
2005 年至 2014 年我在那部剧中扮演泰德·莫斯比 (Ted Mosby)。

00:03:32.259 --> 00:03:37.479
Craig and his buddy Carter Bayes co-created the show.
克雷格和他的好友卡特·贝叶斯共同创作了该剧。

00:03:37.479 --> 00:03:43.219
We did 208 episodes of the show, and we are barreling through watching them all again.
我们已经把这部剧看了 208 集，现在我们正在快速地把它们全部看完。

00:03:43.219 --> 00:03:45.199
And we're coming to the end of season two.
第二季即将结束。

00:03:45.199 --> 00:03:46.199
How is it possible?
怎么可能呢？

00:03:46.199 --> 00:03:47.199
It's incredible.
太不可思议了。

00:03:47.199 --> 00:03:59.280
Today we're talking about episode 21, something borrowed written by Greg Malins, one of our faves who was on the show a couple episodes ago this season.
今天我们要讨论的是第 21 集，这是我们最喜欢的人之一格雷格·马林斯 (Greg Malins) 借来的，他在本季前几集就出现过。

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:02.159
Now, Alec, tell us historically root us.
现在，亚历克，告诉我们历史上的根基。

00:04:02.159 --> 00:04:04.560
When was this, when did this episode air?
这是什么时候的事，这集是什么时候播出的？

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:08.560
Yeah, cast your mind back to May 7th, 2007.
是的，让你的思绪回到 2007 年 5 月 7 日。

00:04:08.560 --> 00:04:10.039
Wow.
哇。

00:04:10.039 --> 00:04:12.360
It's a TV wedding, so it happened in May sweeps.
这是一场电视婚礼，所以它发生在五月份。

00:04:12.360 --> 00:04:13.439
We could have guessed May.
我们本来可以猜到梅。

00:04:13.439 --> 00:04:19.360
May was, May would have been the top most, that would have been number one on the Family Feud guesses.
梅是，梅会是最重要的，这将是家庭世仇猜测中的第一名。

00:04:19.360 --> 00:04:22.120
Yeah, that's, that's crazy, man.
是的，这太疯狂了，伙计。

00:04:22.120 --> 00:04:23.240
This is a good one.
这是一件好事。

00:04:23.240 --> 00:04:24.079
This is a good one.
这是一件好事。

00:04:24.079 --> 00:04:26.920
This begins, this is kind of part one of a two-parter.
这就是开始，这是两人的第一部分。

00:04:26.920 --> 00:04:37.160
And Josh actually said earlier today, like it's kind of part two of a three-parter because it feels like the episode showdown that proceeds this has a few little nuggets planted in it about the wedding.
乔什今天早些时候确实说过，这就像三部曲的第二部分，因为感觉接下来的剧集对决中埋下了一些关于婚礼的小金块。

00:04:37.160 --> 00:04:42.720
You actually get a little glimpse of this wedding, Marshall's in a hat in the wedding very mysteriously.
你实际上可以瞥见这场婚礼，马歇尔在婚礼上戴着帽子，非常神秘。

00:04:42.720 --> 00:04:44.839
Marshall's in a hat, which is great.
马歇尔戴着帽子，这太棒了。

00:04:44.839 --> 00:04:47.399
It led Jordana to say, why is Marshall wearing a hat?
这让乔丹娜说，为什么马歇尔戴着帽子？

00:04:47.399 --> 00:04:49.800
In that weird hat, you know, wait for it?
戴着那顶奇怪的帽子，你知道，等等？

00:04:49.800 --> 00:04:56.920
Yeah, and also isn't showdown where Ted and Robin come in and their spaghetti stained clothes?
是的，泰德和罗宾的衣服也被弄脏了，这不是摊牌吗？

00:04:56.920 --> 00:04:58.000
Yes, yes.
是的，是的。

00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:01.160
They come in and they're covered in spaghetti sauce and they say, we'll tell you that later.
他们进来时，身上沾满了意大利面酱，他们说，我们稍后再告诉你。

00:05:01.160 --> 00:05:09.279
And they mean it, they mean much later because you don't find out until the episode after this, but that's why these three feel like a three part finale in a way of the season.
他们是认真的，他们的意思是很久以后，因为你直到这一集之后才发现，但这就是为什么这三部感觉像是本季的三部分结局。

00:05:09.279 --> 00:05:11.759
Well again, it's mysteries.
好吧，这又是个谜。

00:05:11.759 --> 00:05:13.920
It's little mysteries woven throughout.
这是贯穿始终的小谜团。

00:05:13.920 --> 00:05:22.000
But it also shows to me that you guys were really mapping this thing out and you were really, you wanted everything to count.
但这也向我表明，你们确实在规划这件事，并且你们真的希望一切都重要。

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:27.240
You know, sometimes there's dangling threads or people comment and say, why did this happen?
你知道，有时会有悬而未决的线索或人们评论说，为什么会发生这种情况？

00:05:27.240 --> 00:05:35.160
because in episode, you know, season seven, this happened, and you couldn't account for everything, but I think you guys did an amazing job tying things up and...
因为在第七季中，这件事发生了，你无法解释一切，但我认为你们在把事情联系起来方面做得非常出色......

00:05:35.160 --> 00:05:36.000
Thank you.
谢谢。

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:37.560
Yeah, we took it seriously.
是的，我们很认真地对待它。

00:05:37.560 --> 00:05:44.720
I was very excited because my wife hadn't seen this one a long time and I said, do you, well, I guess it was in showdown, I said this, I said, do you remember why they're covered in pasta sauce?
我很兴奋，因为我的妻子已经很长时间没有看到这个了，我说，你知道吗，好吧，我猜这是在摊牌，我说这个，我说，你还记得为什么他们被意大利面酱覆盖吗？

00:05:44.720 --> 00:05:48.759
And she's like, no, it didn't register for her at all.
她说，不，这根本不适合她。

00:05:48.759 --> 00:05:52.040
And she was like, she didn't think it was weird, it just kind of goes by.
她觉得，她并不觉得这有什么奇怪的，一切就这样过去了。

00:05:52.040 --> 00:05:52.879
Yeah.
是的。

00:05:52.879 --> 00:06:03.199
And then like when we watched, we watched the spoilers, We watched the one we're talking about after this, which is something blue, the finale of season two, and it paid off, and she was very excited, because she was like, that's the pasta sauce.
然后就像我们看的时候一样，我们看了剧透，我们看了之后我们正在谈论的那个，那是蓝色的东西，第二季的结局，它得到了回报，她非常兴奋，因为她说，那就是意大利面酱。

00:06:03.199 --> 00:06:05.480
Okay, now I got it, she completely forgotten.
好吧，现在我明白了，她完全忘记了。

00:06:05.480 --> 00:06:09.160
I forgot about it, and I had actual pasta sauce spilled onto me.
我忘记了，我的意大利面酱洒在了我身上。

00:06:09.160 --> 00:06:10.160
And I forgot about it.
我忘了这件事。

00:06:10.160 --> 00:06:12.639
You're the person who should have had the strongest visceral memory.
你应该是本能记忆力最强的人。

00:06:12.639 --> 00:06:15.600
This vivid, vivid memory, yeah.
这生动、生动的记忆，是的。

00:06:15.600 --> 00:06:23.079
Well, yeah, I mean, I think that that speaks to, like, Carter is a crossword puzzle guy, like me.
嗯，是的，我的意思是，我认为这说明卡特是一个填字游戏爱好者，就像我一样。

00:06:23.079 --> 00:06:28.120
But I think you both have a sense of like, puzzlemanship, is that a word like?
但我想你们都有一种喜欢的感觉，puzzlemanship，是一个“喜欢”这个词吗？

00:06:28.120 --> 00:06:28.959
Puzzlemanship.
解谜术。

00:06:28.959 --> 00:06:30.480
You heard it here first, ladies and gentlemen.
女士们先生们，你们首先在这里听到的。

00:06:30.480 --> 00:06:33.339
You like things kind of falling into place.
你喜欢事情按部就班。

00:06:33.339 --> 00:06:36.040
You like, you got like a good mislead.
你喜欢，你就像一个很好的误导。

00:06:36.040 --> 00:06:37.920
You like, what is going on?
你喜欢，这是怎么回事？

00:06:37.920 --> 00:06:39.399
Why freeze frame?
为什么要冻结帧？

00:06:39.399 --> 00:06:40.439
Let's back up.
让我们备份一下。

00:06:40.439 --> 00:06:41.839
Let's show how we got there.
让我们展示一下我们是如何到达那里的。

00:06:41.839 --> 00:06:42.680
Yeah.
是的。

00:06:42.680 --> 00:06:44.120
We love telling stories like that.
我们喜欢讲这样的故事。

00:06:44.120 --> 00:06:50.160
We looked at these seasons, kind of like novels, kind of like you could jump around and really everything should refer to something else.
我们审视了这些季节，有点像小说，有点像你可以跳来跳去，实际上一切都应该指其他东西。

00:06:50.160 --> 00:06:52.480
Everything should be there for a reason if it possibly can be.
如果可能的话，一切事物的存在都应该有其原因。

00:06:52.480 --> 00:07:09.120
And we really looked at a season as one big organism rather than this series of standalone things, which like we said at the beginning of season two, the network would have probably been happier at the time if we had done standalone episodes that were very digestible, that required little thought.
我们确实将一季视为一个大有机体，而不是这一系列独立的事物，就像我们在第二季开始时所说的那样，如果我们制作非常容易理解的独立剧集，那么网络当时可能会更高兴，这不需要太多思考。

00:07:09.120 --> 00:07:11.279
You don't have to have seen them in any particular order.
您不必按任何特定顺序查看它们。

00:07:11.279 --> 00:07:12.800
It's just not how we write.
这不是我们写作的方式。

00:07:12.800 --> 00:07:14.240
They asked us to do more of that.
他们要求我们做更多这样的事情。

00:07:14.240 --> 00:07:18.959
And we kind of said, we'll try knowing that we were not going to try and that we can't.
我们说过，我们会尝试，但我们知道我们不会尝试，也不能尝试。

00:07:18.959 --> 00:07:20.959
We could only write it this way.
我们只能这样写。

00:07:20.959 --> 00:07:28.360
And I have to say rewatching it feels very vindicating for that decision because it's very fun to watch like this again, right?
我不得不说，重新观看它感觉非常证明了这个决定，因为再次观看这样的视频非常有趣，对吧？

00:07:28.360 --> 00:07:31.560
It's very fun to go back and go, this is why everything was here.
来来去去非常有趣，这就是一切都在这里的原因。

00:07:31.560 --> 00:07:47.079
But I think the sense of payoff that is tied to the longevity and serialized nature of the show is why people keep coming back to the show, like it's some sort of holy shrine because it rewards your fidelity to it.
但我认为与该剧的长久性和连续性相关的回报感是人们不断回到该剧的原因，就像它是某种圣殿一样，因为它奖励你对它的忠诚。

00:07:47.079 --> 00:07:48.639
Like the more you watch it.
越看越喜欢。

00:07:48.639 --> 00:07:50.560
It's like, you know, there's- It is, it's little clues.
就像，你知道，有——是的，这是一些线索。

00:07:50.560 --> 00:07:53.560
I'm gonna, I know what that clue is gonna, I know what the payoff's gonna be to that clue.
我会，我知道那条线索会是什么，我知道这条线索的回报是什么。

00:07:53.560 --> 00:07:54.759
And people like that.
人们喜欢这样。

00:07:54.759 --> 00:08:07.920
There's a thing, I think this is from the Bhagavad Gita, forgive me if it's not, but there's some line essentially from like Vedic something where it says, the first taste of ego is sweet, but its fruits are bitter.
有件事，我认为这是来自《薄伽梵歌》，如果不是，请原谅我，但有一些诗句本质上来自吠陀经，它说，自我的初尝是甜的，但它的果实是苦的。

00:08:07.920 --> 00:08:11.279
And the first taste of God is bitter, but its fruits are sweet.
神的初尝是苦的，但它的果子却是甜的。

